B.RW=s(["Continuous or near-continuous focal clonic jerking, classically of a hand or face","Awareness usually preserved","Can persist for hours to years despite treatment","Causes include Rasmussen encephalitis, stroke, tumor, and metabolic derangement (e.g. non-ketotic hyperglycemia)"],t.s)
B.a1h=new A.cd("Epilepsia partialis continua","Rare / genetic syndrome","A rare form of focal motor status epilepticus with continuous, rhythmic clonic jerking of a body part, sometimes lasting days to years, usually with preserved awareness.",B.RW,"May show focal epileptiform discharges time-locked to the jerking, but scalp EEG is often unremarkable given the small cortical generator.","A normal scalp EEG does not exclude epilepsia partialis continua \u2014 the diagnosis is primarily clinical.",!0)
B.S2=s(["Clusters of brief (seconds to a minute), stereotyped hypermotor/hyperkinetic seizures during sleep","Onset usually in childhood, often with a family history","Frequently confused with NREM parasomnias (sleepwalking, night terrors)","Preserved awareness is common, distinguishing it from typical parasomnias"],t.s)
B.a0V=new A.cd("Sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy (SHE; formerly ADNFLE)","Rare / genetic syndrome","Renamed sleep-related hypermotor epilepsy in 2016. The familial form is linked to nicotinic receptor genes (CHRNA4, CHRNB2), though most cases are sporadic. It causes brief, clustered hypermotor seizures during sleep, frequently misdiagnosed as a parasomnia.",B.S2,"Scalp EEG is frequently normal or non-localizing given the frontal, often mesial, seizure onset.","A strong family history of stereotyped nocturnal events favors ADNFLE over an isolated parasomnia.",!0)
